<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Ravenroot OpenAI-compatible model adapter — an OPTIONAL, SEPARATELY INSTALLED artifact.

    THIS MODULE IS DELIBERATELY NOT A MEMBER OF ravenroot/pom.xml's <modules>, AND MUST NEVER BE
    ADDED TO IT. Same rule, same three checks and same reason as ravenroot-adapter-anthropic, which
    is the precedent this module copies rather than reinterprets:

        "Move this class to an optional, separately-installed adapter artifact with its own Maven
         coordinates, excluded from the release reactor - a Maven profile of ravenroot-distribution
         is not sufficient."

    Ravenroot's qualification as an upstream component under EU AI Act recital 89, rather than a
    provider of an AI system under art. 3(3), rests on the DEFAULT distribution shipping no concrete,
    registered ModelProvider under ADR 0017. This module contains a concrete ModelProvider.
    Its own groupId (ai.ravenroot.adapters, not ai.ravenroot) and its absence from the release
    reactor are what make "additional artifact, not part of the default distribution" a fact about
    the build rather than a sentence in a document.

    The enforcing checks live in ravenroot-distribution: DefaultDistributionModelAdapterBoundaryTest
    (phase test), ReleaseArtifactModelAdapterBoundaryIT (phase verify) and ReleaseArtifactBoundaryGate
    (phase package, via exec-maven-plugin — the one that survives -DskipTests).

    Consequence for building it: the reactor must be installed first, because this project resolves
    its Ravenroot dependencies from the local repository rather than from a reactor sibling.

        ./dev.sh          <- performs this step and every other one, selecting a JDK 21..25 itself

    The command above is the recommended approach: dev.sh finds a supported JDK instead of
    depending on an absolute path from the machine that wrote this line. For anyone who wants to
    run the sequence manually with JAVA_HOME already in range:

        mvn -f ravenroot/pom.xml install -pl ravenroot-core -am
        mvn -f ravenroot-adapter-openai-compatible/pom.xml clean verify

    See docs/architecture/model-provider-adapters.md for where an adapter may live and why.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>ai.ravenroot.adapters</groupId>
    <artifactId>ravenroot-adapter-openai-compatible</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-alpha.1</version>
    <name>Ravenroot OpenAI-compatible Model Adapter</name>
    <description>
        Optional ModelProvider adapter for OpenAI-compatible /v1/chat/completions endpoints — a local
        Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM, or a hosted provider that speaks the same shape. Operator-key
        credential path (answer A3). Not part of the Ravenroot default distribution.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <project.reporting.outputEncoding>UTF-8</project.reporting.outputEncoding>
        <ravenroot.version>0.1.0-alpha.1</ravenroot.version>
        <junit.version>6.1.2</junit.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!--
            The SPI itself: ModelProvider, ModelRequest, ModelResponse, CredentialResolver,
            SecretValue, and the bounded JSON reader/writer (PayloadJson, PayloadValue,
            PayloadLimits) this adapter reads and writes the wire with.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>ai.ravenroot</groupId>
            <artifactId>ravenroot-application-api</artifactId>
            <version>${ravenroot.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            EgressHttpClients and BoundedBodyHandlers. An llm-prompt node is
            graph-driven egress, so this adapter obeys SEC-10 by USING the SEC-10 client rather than
            by resembling it. ravenroot-adapter-anthropic's pom records at length why its first
            revision — a vendor SDK plus application-api only — was rejected: the SDK's own client
            followed a 307 and carried the operator key and the rendered prompt to a second host.
            Not repeated here; read it there.

            provided, not compile, and that scope is an assertion rather than a saving: the only way
            to install this adapter is to hand it to ModelProviderRegistry, itself a ravenroot-core
            class, so any JVM that can register this provider already has core on its classpath.

            KNOWN and deliberate, inherited unchanged from the sibling: this drags tinkergraph-gremlin
            and cel-standalone onto the compile and test classpath for one utility class and one body
            handler that need neither. The right shape is for the SEC-10 egress constructor to live in
            the published SPI artifact — docs/architecture/model-provider-adapters.md, "What the two
            clients can actually share", point 1.

            provided is also on the TEST classpath, which is what lets this module run the acceptance
            test that drives a real llm-prompt node through BehaviorRegistry against this adapter.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>ai.ravenroot</groupId>
            <artifactId>ravenroot-core</artifactId>
            <version>${ravenroot.version}</version>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <version>${junit.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.6.1</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>enforce-java-version</id>
                        <phase>validate</phase>
                        <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <rules>
                                <!-- Same supported JDK window as the reactor. -->
                                <requireJavaVersion>
                                    <version>[21,26)</version>
                                    <message>Ravenroot builds on JDK 21 through 25; JDK 26 is not a supported build platform.</message>
                                </requireJavaVersion>
                            </rules>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.15.0</version>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.5.6</version>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
